<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ostsiedlung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as East settlement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ostsiedlung, East settlement, was the medieval migration of ethnic Germans into areas populated by Slavic, Baltic and Uralic peoples, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. Larger treks of settlers moved east from the mid-12th century, often invited by local princes, and towns were granted German town law, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> laws; the movement ended in the beginning of the 14th century. In the 20th century German nationalists, most notoriously the Nazis, exploited it for territorial claims, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was reversed east of the Oder as Germans were driven out.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung.jpg" alt="Ostsiedlung" width="330" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B10_047-S._IMPERIUM_ROMANO-GERMANICUM_oder_DEUTSCHLAND_MIT_SEINEN_ANGRÄNTZENDEN_KÖNIGREICHEN_UND_PROVINCIEN_Neulich_entworffen_und_theils_gezeichnet_durch_IULIUM_REICHELT_Chur_Pfaltz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Ostsiedlung followed a large rise in population across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the High Middle Ages. From the 11th to the 13th centuries the population of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> grew from about four to twelve million, and arable land was expanded at the expense of forest. Demand for land still could not be met. Landless younger sons of noble families, who could not inherit property, were among those who sought new holdings on the edges of the Empire, especially after the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><p>East of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> lived Slavic peoples known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> or Elbe Slavs, who seldom formed large political units. A Slavic revolt in 983 ended imperial rule and Christian missions between the Elbe and the Baltic, and the area stayed under Polabian tribes into the 12th century. A call for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Wends in 1108, offering land to new settlers, had no noticeable effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first settlers, mostly Flemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, arrived in 1124 and settled south of the Eider. The conquest of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagri">Wagri</a> in 1139 and the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> in 1143 followed. In 1152 many Flemish and Dutch people were brought to marshland east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> settled Mecklenburg with Flemish people. Dutch settlers were valued as hydraulic engineers who drained land with networks of ditches.</p><p>Settlement spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, where German traders, miners, farmers and monks arrived and Germans came to make up almost a quarter of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. In 1149 Géza II invited German settlers to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, where the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> became the official term for local Germans in 1231. Most settlers were Germans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, Scots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloons">Walloons</a> and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>. Towns were founded or enlarged with regular street plans and central market places, and Cistercian monasteries joined faith and settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Settlers brought heavier ploughs, half-timbered building and hard-fired pottery, and Slavic place names such as those ending in -ow or -witz remain common in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Assimilation ran in both directions: Germans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> were assimilated into the Polish population over about two centuries, while small Sorbian communities were absorbed by German speakers. About 25 per cent of German family names are estimated to be of Slavic origin.</p><p>The movement slowed after 1300. Explanations include the worsening climate from about 1300, the agricultural crisis of the mid-14th century and the plague of 1347, though no single cause can be weighed against the others. In the 19th and 20th centuries nationalists used the Ostsiedlung to support the idea of a German drive to the east, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was reversed east of the Oder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ostsiedlung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lords who needed settlers to clear forests and marshes, or to farm the lands east of the Elbe opened by German settlement (the <b>Ostsiedlung</b>), offered them easier terms.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li></ul></section>
